// A reserved word used as a name says so, rather than leaving the reader with a bare token name.
//
// bison's own message is `unexpected BASE, expecting IDENTIFIER` — it names the TOKEN and
// never says what the token IS, so it reads like a parser fault. That made
// three of these (`base`, `type`, `slot`) read as possible compiler bugs when they were hit. `type` and
// `slot` are contextual keywords now and name bindings freely; `base` is one of the 83 that do not, and
// this is the message those get.
//
// ⚠️ The note is gated on the parser having WANTED an identifier. `unexpected ELSE` in a malformed `if`
// is not a naming mistake, and a note there would be noise — so the gate is part of the claim, not an
// optimisation.
fn int32 main()
{
    isize base = 1;
    return cast<int32>(base);
}
